 "stimulus_html": "For the following exercises, fill in the blank with <i>the same as</i> or <i>different from</i> to make the statement true. ",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 544532,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Unlike  in a Hamilton cycle, the vertex where the Hamilton path begins is _________ the vertex where the Hamilton path ends.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "A Hamilton path does not need to begin and end on the same vertex."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "different from"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 544533,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "If a sequence of vertices represents a Hamilton path, the number of vertices listed should be _______ the number of vertices in the whole graph.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "A Hamilton path does not need to begin and end on the same vertex. It does need to visit every vertex exactly once. While making that happen, the number of vertices listed will always be the same as the number of vertices in the graph."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "the same as"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 544534,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "To determine if a graph has a Hamilton path, use a method that is _________ the method used to determine if a graph has an Euler trail.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "A path that visits each vertex exactly once is a Hamilton path. Recall that a path is a walk that has no repeated vertices or edges. An Euler trail uses every edge exactly once. It is only logical that the methods of finding them would be different since you have different goals."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "different from"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 544535,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "If a graph with a bridge has a Hamilton path, the starting vertex should be on the side of the bridge that is ________ the side of the bridge with the ending vertex.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "If a graph has a bridge, the start of a Hamilton path will be on one side of the bridge, and the ending vertex will be on the other side of the bridge. You will avoid crossing the bridge until you have no other choice."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "different from"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
